B.cLp=new A.u("Rooms are private to their members. Contributions retain their author and timestamp.\n\nDeleting your account deletes your profile, and every room nobody else is in, with its songs. A room other people are in is handed over or closed first. Songs you started and takes you shared in other people's rooms stay there, because their bands cannot re-record them. Your name comes off them, and words you wrote are deleted. Anything you recorded but never shared is deleted with everything else.\n\nMessages\n\nA message to one person is seen by the two of you; a room's thread by everybody in the room, including people who join later. Every line you wrote can be taken back. Your people \u2014 connections and everybody you share a room with \u2014 can see whether you have the app open, unless you turn on Appear away; nobody else can, anybody you have blocked included, and it is not recorded. Deleting your account deletes every message you sent.\n\nCalls\n\nA call between people in a room runs through LiveKit, which carries the sound and the picture while the call is on. Calls are never recorded. While you are in one the app notes that you are there, so the room can show who is in it; it stops showing you within a minute of leaving. Before your first call you say the month and year you were born, once, because calls are for people 18 and over for now. Nobody else sees it. For some answers the month is not kept, only that an answer was given.\n\nYour code\n\nYour code opens your name, your picture and what you play, so somebody you have met can ask to add you. Make a new code and the old one opens nobody.\n\nAsking the app about a song\n\nYour question and what the app worked out about the song \u2014 key, chords, sections, the words, who played what \u2014 go to a language model to write the answer. The recording never does. The privacy policy, below, says which one.\n\nWhere your audio goes\n\nAnalysing a recording sends it off this device. It is uploaded to CoLabRoom's storage, then passed to a GPU service that separates the instruments and a chord-detection service that reads the harmony. If there is singing, the isolated vocal is sent to OpenAI's transcription API to work out the words.\n\nAnalyses are cached by the recording's fingerprint. If the exact same file is analysed again \u2014 by you, or by anyone else who has that same file \u2014 the second one reuses the first result instead of processing it again. Nobody can reach an analysis without already having the recording it came from.\n\nYour recordings stay private to your rooms. Removing a recording from a song deletes it and everything derived from it, and deleting your account removes all of it.",null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null)
